{"id":"6b7aff558d4a881e0048830bf8f0c3209ab9ad44","axis":"silent","repo":"apache/camel","sha":"6b7aff558d4a881e0048830bf8f0c3209ab9ad44","parsed":{"files":[{"path":"docs/user-manual/modules/ROOT/pages/camel-jbang-projects.adoc","lines":["@@ -212,6 +212,22 @@ int exit = new ProjectExporter(context).export(request);"," To find the dependencies of the integration, the export boots it silently, the same way as `camel run` does."," This is done by a `DependencyResolver`, which `camel-jbang-core` provides, so it must be on the classpath as well (or you set your own resolver on the `ExportContext`)."," ","+The export keeps to the request and leaves the JVM as it is, so an application can export many times, and several exports can run at the same time:","+","+* The run settings of the silent boot and the project being built are kept in the work directory of the request (`request.workDir`).","+It is the `.camel-jbang` directory of the current directory by default, as with `camel export`, and it is deleted and created again on each export: give each export that runs at the same time a directory of its own.","+* The export does not configure logging: it logs as the application has configured.","+* The export reads no system properties for its options.","+`camel export` sets what its system properties configure (such as `camel.jbang.camelSpringBootVersion` or `camel.jbang.repos`) on the request before it exports.","+* The extra default Maven repositories are an option of the request, used for the downloads and in the `pom.xml` of the project.","+When they are not set, they are the ones of the `camel.extra.repos` system property, as with the other commands:","++","+[source,java]","+----","+// download from, and add to the pom.xml, this repository (and none of camel.extra.repos)","+request.mavenResolver = new MavenResolver(true, null, false, \"acme=https://repo.acme.com/maven2\");","+----","+"," == Gathering list of dependencies"," "," List the Camel components and JARs required t